y(x, y)));\n  const neighborCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {\n    for (const [nx, ny] of neighborsOf(x, y)) {\n      const key = cellKey(nx, ny);\n      neighborCounts.set(key, (neighborCounts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of neighborCounts) {\n    if (willBeAliveNext(count, liveKeys.has(key))) {\n      next.push(parseKey(key));\n    }\n  }\n\n  return next.sort(compareCells);\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784969095164},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The file looks good. Now let me finalize the report.\n\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\nSame calculation as before because only names changed.\n\n`willBeAliveNext`:\n- Constants: `3`, `2`, `2` → 3\n- Bindings: `neighborCount`, `isCurrentlyAlive` → 2\n- Invocations: 0 → 0\n- Conditionals: `||`, `&&` → 2 → 8\n- Loops: 0 → 0\n- Assignments: 0 → 0\n\n**Total Mass: 13** (unchanged)\n\n`nextGeneration` mass is unchanged (same number of bindings/invocations/conditionals/loops/assignments; only identifier names changed).\n\nMass Change: 13 → 13 (delta 0) for the renamed function; overall file mass unchanged.\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Naming: Renamed `survives` → `willBeAliveNext` because the function determines whether *any* cell (live or dead) will be alive in the next generation, not just whether a live cell survives.\n- Naming: Renamed parameter `isAlive` → `isCurrentlyAlive` to distinguish current state from next-generation state.\n- Naming: Renamed local `live` → `liveKeys` to clarify it stores string keys, not Cell objects.\n\nBenefits:\n- Function name now accurately describes both survival and birth behavior.\n- Parameter name removes ambiguity about which generation's state is being tested.\n- Local variable name clarifies that the Set contains string keys for identity lookup.\n- All tests remain green.\n\nI should present this clearly and then return to the parent agent.","thinkin